  <!-- ──────────────── OVERVIEW ──────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">Overview</div>
    <p>I spent a week in Lombok using Kuta as a base and doing a mix of beach days, scooter rides, waterfalls, surfing, and short hikes. Lombok feels slower than Bali. Roads are quieter, accommodation is cheaper, and it is easier to find empty beaches outside the main tourist areas.</p>
    <p>The south coast around Kuta works well for independent travellers. Most places are within 20–40 minutes by scooter, food is cheap, and there are enough cafes and guesthouses without feeling overdeveloped. Senggigi and the Gili Islands are more resort-oriented, but still easy to visit if you have extra time.</p>
    <p>I averaged around 600,000 IDR per day including scooter rental, private room accommodation, local food, and activities. Costs rise quickly if you stay on the Gilis or book private drivers every day.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ──────────────── NEED TO KNOW ──────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">Need to Know</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Most travellers arrive through Lombok International Airport near Praya. The airport is around 30–40 minutes from Kuta by car.</li>
      <li>Dry season from May to September is the best time to visit. October to March can bring heavy afternoon rain and rougher seas.</li>
      <li>A scooter is the easiest way to explore southern Lombok. Roads are generally good around Kuta, but some beach access roads are steep or rough.</li>
      <li>ATMs are common in Kuta and Senggigi but less reliable on smaller islands and in rural areas. Carry cash for warungs and local entrance fees.</li>
      <li>Mobile reception is good around Kuta and Senggigi. Telkomsel had the best coverage once outside town.</li>
      <li>Mount Rinjani hikes normally require advance booking during peak season. Weather cancellations happen regularly during the wet season.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ──────────────── WHAT TO DO ──────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">What to Do</div>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Tanjung Aan Beach</strong> is one of the easiest beach stops from Kuta. White sand, calm water, and a few simple beach cafes. Around 20 minutes by scooter from town. Go early before the day-trip crowds arrive.</li>
      <li><strong>Bukit Merese</strong> is the main sunset hill near Kuta. Short 10–15 minute walk from the parking area. The views over Tanjung Aan and the coastline are better than the walk itself. Bring water — there is little shade.</li>
      <li><strong>Selong Belanak Beach</strong> is the easiest surf beach for beginners in southern Lombok. Board rental and lessons are available directly on the sand. Around 45–60 minutes from Kuta depending on traffic and road conditions.</li>
      <li><strong>Benang Stokel and Benang Kelambu Waterfalls</strong> make a good inland day trip. The waterfalls sit around 1.5–2 hours north of Kuta by scooter. Expect short jungle walks, stairs, and wet paths. Entry and parking combined were roughly 50,000–80,000 IDR.</li>
      <li><strong>Pink Beach</strong> on the southeast coast is quieter than the beaches around Kuta. The drive takes around 2 hours each way, partly on rough roads. Better as a full-day scooter ride or private driver trip.</li>
      <li><strong>Gili Islands</strong> are easy to visit from Lombok. Fast boats run from Bangsal Harbour to Gili Trawangan, Gili Air, and Gili Meno. Gili Air felt the most balanced — quieter than Gili Trawangan but with enough cafes and guesthouses for a few nights.</li>
      <li><strong>Mount Rinjani</strong> is Lombok's biggest draw for hikers. Most treks are 2 days / 1 night or 3 days / 2 nights. Summit routes involve long steep climbs, cold overnight temperatures, and early alpine starts. Even fit hikers should expect a tough climb above 2,500 m.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

