  <!-- ──────────────── GETTING THERE ──────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">Getting There</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Flights from Bali to Lombok take around 40 minutes. Budget airlines regularly operate the route for around 600,000–1,500,000 IDR return depending on season.</li>
      <li>Public ferries from Padang Bai in Bali to Lembar Port in Lombok take 4–6 hours and cost roughly 70,000–90,000 IDR per person. Slow but cheap.</li>
      <li>Fast boats from Bali to Lombok or the Gili Islands usually take 1.5–3 hours depending on sea conditions and departure point. Prices vary between 300,000–800,000 IDR.</li>
      <li>Lombok International Airport is connected to Jakarta, Bali, Surabaya, Kuala Lumpur, and Singapore.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ──────────────── GETTING AROUND ──────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">Getting Around</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Scooter rental is the standard way to get around southern Lombok. Expect around 80,000–120,000 IDR per day for a basic automatic scooter.</li>
      <li>Fuel is cheap and easy to find around Kuta. Most local roadside bottles cost around 15,000–20,000 IDR per litre.</li>
      <li>Grab and Gojek are limited outside airport areas and larger towns. Do not rely on rideshare apps once outside Kuta or Senggigi.</li>
      <li>Private drivers are common for waterfall trips and airport transfers. Full-day hire normally costs around 600,000–900,000 IDR.</li>
      <li>Shuttle vans run between Kuta, Senggigi, Bangsal Harbour, and the airport. Slower than private transport but useful if travelling solo.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ──────────────── WHERE TO STAY ──────────────── -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-heading">Where to Stay</div>
    <ul>
      <li>Kuta is the best overall base for independent travellers. Plenty of guesthouses, surf hostels, cafes, and scooter rental shops. Dorm beds start around 120,000–180,000 IDR and private rooms around 300,000–700,000 IDR.</li>
      <li>Senggigi works better for shorter holidays or travellers wanting easier beach access without scooters. Accommodation is more resort-focused. Mid-range hotels generally start around 700,000 IDR.</li>
      <li>Gili Air and Gili Trawangan suit slower beach stays and snorkelling trips. Prices rise noticeably during weekends and holidays. Budget bungalows start around 350,000 IDR.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

